+A net weight scales the whole net, so a failing sink of a high-fanout net
+that sits inside the net's bounding box is not pulled toward its driver.
+With `-timing_driven_pin_pull`, `gpl` also adds a two-pin attraction
+between each failing sink and its driver. The sink's slack is read on the
+netlist the virtual `repair_design` buffered. The attraction is a spring in
+the initial placement, which is placed again after a timing analysis
+(`-timing_driven_pin_pull_initial_rounds` times), and the weighted-average
+length of the two-pin net in Nesterov. A failing connection pulls
+`-timing_driven_pin_pull_strength` times as hard as the heavier of its two
+cells' other nets together. Its weight grows at every update it still
+fails (`-timing_driven_pin_pull_growth`, capped by
+`-timing_driven_pin_pull_max`) and is kept once it meets timing. The
+pulls are not nets and do not change the reported HPWL.
+
